lessons.push(
{unit:2,title:'Vectors, tensors, and a Python refresher',goal:'Track shapes and compute a predictor with explicit numerical operations.',paragraphs:[
'A scalar is one number. A vector is an ordered list of numbers, and a matrix is a rectangular arrangement. A tensor generalizes the idea to more axes. The shape describes the size of each axis, not what it means. A batch of eight examples with three features has shape eight by three. Document which axis represents examples and which represents features before writing a multiplication.',
'A dot product multiplies corresponding entries and adds the results. For features two and three, and weights four and minus one, the prediction is eight minus three, or five. A bias of one raises it to six. The weights describe sensitivity to each feature. Changing the order of features without changing the weights changes the meaning of the predictor, even when the shapes still match.',
'Matrix multiplication applies these dot products systematically. A batch with shape eight by three multiplied by weights with shape three by two produces eight by two outputs. The shared dimension of three is summed over. Elementwise multiplication is a different operation. Broadcasting can silently repeat values across an axis; a program that runs without a shape error can still compute the wrong loss.',
'In Python, lists hold ordered values and dictionaries map keys to values. The zip function pairs corresponding entries; sum combines products. A list comprehension expresses a short loop that builds a list. Functions make assumptions explicit, and assertions check them. Our small example checks equal lengths because zip otherwise stops at the shorter input. An array library can vectorize this calculation, but the scalar definition remains the reference.',
'Numerical representation also matters. Floating-point values approximate many real numbers; different addition orders may produce slightly different results. Use a tolerance when checking numerical equality. Lower precision can save memory while increasing rounding error. Normalizing a feature means computing a transformation, such as subtracting a training-set mean and dividing by a training-set standard deviation, then reusing those training statistics for evaluation.',
'Before the lab, predict a dot product of five and a biased prediction of six. Run both scenarios and observe that the feature computation stays fixed while the learning rate changes the update. Trace one operation with pencil, speech, or the code listing. If the output surprises you, check the feature order, the bias, and whether multiplication is elementwise or a dot product. These checks transfer directly to neural-network tensors.'
],code:'def predict(features, weights, bias):\n    assert len(features) == len(weights)\n    return sum(x * w for x, w in zip(features, weights)) + bias\n\nassert predict([2, 3], [4, -1], 1) == 6\nprint("prediction:", predict([2, 3], [4, -1], 1))',question:'A batch has shape 8 by 3 and its weight matrix has shape 3 by 2. What is the output shape?',options:['3 by 3','8 by 2','2 by 8'],correct:1,explanation:'Each of eight examples produces two dot products, each summing three features. The shared feature dimension disappears from the output shape.',prompt:'Trace the dot product and bias aloud. Then describe how you would detect a swapped feature column that does not cause a shape error.',rubric:'Compute 2 times 4 plus 3 times minus 1 equals 5, then add bias 1. Use feature names, a hand-computed reference example, and shape assertions; shape checks alone cannot detect changed semantics.',recall:'In three days, compute a new two-feature prediction without notes and explain one broadcasting mistake.'},
{unit:2,title:'Probability, loss, and gradients',goal:'Connect probabilities and the chain rule to a verified parameter update.',paragraphs:[
'Probability represents uncertainty within a model. Conditional probability asks about an outcome given specified information. It is not the probability that an entire fluent explanation is correct. A categorical distribution assigns nonnegative probabilities that sum to one. Softmax turns real-valued scores into such a distribution by exponentiating and normalizing them. Subtracting the largest score first gives the same probabilities with safer numerical computation.',
'For a correct target assigned probability one half, negative log likelihood is approximately zero point six nine three using natural logarithms. Assigning probability one quarter doubles that loss to about one point three eight six. Confident wrong answers receive a large penalty. Averaging these losses over examples defines cross-entropy training in a common classification setting. The average can conceal a small group on which the model fails badly.',
'A derivative measures a local rate of change. A gradient collects derivatives with respect to several parameters. For a prediction equal to input times weight, and loss equal to half the squared prediction error, the derivative with respect to weight is the input multiplied by the difference between prediction and target. At input two, weight one, and target six, this derivative is minus eight. The negative sign means a small increase in weight should lower the loss.',
'Gradient descent subtracts learning rate times gradient. A rate of zero point one therefore changes weight one to one point eight. The new loss is two point eight eight, down from eight. A rate of one changes the weight to nine and makes the loss seventy-two. A direction that is locally helpful does not justify an arbitrarily long step. Inspect the objective after an update rather than assuming that an optimizer always improves it.',
'The chain rule connects intermediate derivatives. The derivative of loss with respect to prediction is prediction minus target; the derivative of prediction with respect to weight is input. Multiplying them gives the weight derivative. If the same parameter influences several paths, add their contributions. Backpropagation organizes these calculations; the optimizer is the separate procedure that uses the resulting gradients.',
'Check the derivative independently with a finite difference: evaluate the loss slightly above and below the current weight, subtract, and divide by twice the perturbation. The lab uses a small perturbation and compares this estimate with minus eight. Do not make the perturbation so tiny that rounding dominates. Predict both learning-rate scenarios before running them, and explain why the larger update is a failure of step size rather than evidence that the derivative was wrong.'
],question:'The gradient is minus 8 at weight 1. What does gradient descent with rate 0.1 do?',options:['Changes the weight to 1.8','Changes the weight to 0.2','Leaves the weight fixed'],correct:0,explanation:'Subtracting 0.1 times minus 8 adds 0.8. The gradient gives a local direction; a finite difference independently checks the sign and magnitude.',prompt:'Derive the update in words and explain why rate 1 overshoots. Contrast a class probability with evidence that a factual answer is supported.',rubric:'Gradient equals input times prediction error: 2 times minus 4 equals minus 8. Rates 0.1 and 1 produce weights 1.8 and 9. Losses are 2.88 and 72. Probability is conditional on the model and input, not a factual guarantee.',recall:'After three days, derive the gradient at weight 4 and predict which direction lowers the loss.'},
{unit:3,title:'Train a small neural network',goal:'Follow a forward pass, backpropagation, and optimization in a nonlinear model.',paragraphs:[
'A linear model can only express relationships allowed by its features. Stacking linear layers without nonlinearities still gives a linear transformation. A neural network introduces nonlinear functions between learned transformations. Our lab uses a hidden layer with four tanh units. Each unit forms a weighted input plus a bias and squashes it between minus one and one. A learned weighted sum of those hidden values produces the prediction.',
'The training examples pair inputs with their squares. This curved relationship is a useful counterexample to the idea that more optimization can repair an unsuitable linear model. We include negative and positive inputs, train on a small symmetric set, and hold out intermediate points. A constant or linear baseline gives a comparison. The task is intentionally tiny so every parameter, input, and prediction can be inspected.',
'The forward pass computes hidden activations, then an output, then half the squared error. For an output weight, multiply output error by the associated hidden activation. For a hidden input weight, propagate error through the output weight, multiply by one minus the squared tanh activation, and then multiply by input. Compute all these derivatives using the same old parameters before applying an update.',
'Initialization breaks symmetry. If hidden units begin identically and receive identical gradients, they can remain duplicates instead of learning different features. The lab uses fixed, different starting values for reproducibility. A fixed initialization makes comparisons controlled; it does not establish that a result will hold for every seed. On larger tasks, repeat experiments with several seeds and report variability rather than selecting the best run silently.',
'An epoch is one pass over the training examples. A minibatch estimates a gradient from part of the training set; a full-batch update uses every example in that set. The lab performs a fixed number of full-batch steps. Training loss indicates how well the parameters fit those examples. Held-out error answers a different question: whether the learned curve predicts inputs that were not used in the updates.',
'Run the baseline and inspect training and held-out losses along with the predictions. Then choose the failure scenario, which introduces an inconsistent target. Predict whether fitting that point helps the held-out curve. If learning stalls, check gradient signs, nonlinear activation derivatives, feature scale, and the learning rate. If loss improves only on training examples, investigate the data and generalization rather than merely adding more steps.'
],question:'What does backpropagation compute?',options:['A train/test split','Gradients of the objective through the computation graph','A guarantee that the model generalizes'],correct:1,explanation:'Backpropagation applies the chain rule to compute gradients efficiently. An optimizer changes the parameters, and a separate evaluation measures generalization.',prompt:'Trace the derivative for one hidden weight. Explain why the output weights must not be updated before the hidden gradients use them.',rubric:'Multiply output error, the old output weight, the tanh derivative, and the input. Compute a consistent gradient at one parameter state. Compare held-out predictions against a baseline before claiming usefulness.',recall:'In three days, explain why two linear layers alone do not add nonlinear expressiveness.'},
{unit:3,title:'Generalization, baselines, and diagnosis',goal:'Separate optimization success from useful performance on new examples.',paragraphs:[
'Generalization means performance transfers to examples representing the intended future use. Training performance measures fit to observed data. Validation guides choices such as learning rate, model size, and stopping time. A final test set is reserved for an evaluation after those choices. Repeatedly choosing changes because they improve the test set turns that set into part of the selection process.',
'A baseline makes a result interpretable. For regression, predicting the training mean can be a useful minimal baseline. For an imbalanced classification task, a system that always predicts the majority class may achieve high accuracy while never identifying the rare event. Match the baseline and metric to the decision. Report the class distribution and a confusion matrix when overall accuracy hides important error types.',
'Overfitting occurs when adaptation to training details does not transfer. A flexible model can follow label noise. Regularization adds constraints or penalties, early stopping chooses a checkpoint using validation behavior, and more representative data may improve the evidence available. These tools have tradeoffs: excessive regularization can erase a real pattern, and a poor validation split can select the wrong stopping point.',
'In our controlled lab, one scenario changes the training target at the center while keeping held-out targets on the original square curve. The network now sees conflicting evidence about the underlying process. Examine both losses and individual predictions; do not assume that every mislabeled example must produce one particular metric change. Identify whether the error is systematic, concentrated near the corrupted point, or spread across the curve.',
'Distribution shift changes the conditions under which a model is used. A speech model may meet a new microphone, a support assistant may meet a new product version, and a video model may meet unfamiliar compression. A random split from yesterday does not automatically test tomorrow. Grouped and time-based splits can answer more relevant questions when customers, scenes, or collection periods are related.',
'Keep an experiment record with the dataset version, split rule, initialization, objective, hyperparameters, and evaluation. Change one factor when diagnosing a failure. Compare a small model against a larger one on the same held-out set, and report failures as well as averages. For this lab, save your baseline and corrupted-target predictions in the notes and propose a test that could distinguish a model-capacity problem from a data-quality problem.'
],question:'You repeatedly select settings using the final test set. What has changed?',options:['The test set now influences model selection','The training objective becomes unbiased','Leakage is impossible because gradients never use test rows'],correct:0,explanation:'Selection itself can adapt to a test set even without direct gradient updates. Use validation for tuning and reserve independent evaluation for the final assessment.',prompt:'Compare training and held-out results in both lab scenarios. Propose a baseline, a grouped split, and one additional diagnostic that would change your conclusion.',rubric:'Discuss separate training and held-out evidence, individual residuals, a mean or linear baseline, and a split matching future use. A lower training loss alone is insufficient. Label corruption is a data issue even when model flexibility affects its impact.',recall:'After one week, explain how a model can improve an average metric while becoming worse for a rare group.'},
{unit:4,title:'Build a documented dataset',goal:'Treat provenance, permitted use, and transformations as part of the dataset.',paragraphs:[
'A dataset is a versioned collection of observations plus the information needed to interpret and use them. For each source, record what was collected, by whom, when, for what purpose, and under what stated permissions. Also record what was excluded. A file being downloadable is an observation about access, not a complete answer about authorization for every possible reuse.',
'Start with an intended task and population. A support-question dataset might cover installation problems from new customers during a particular product release. Collecting only successful conversations will underrepresent failures. Sampling only active forum users may miss people who never post. A dataset statement should describe these boundaries so downstream users can recognize mismatches rather than treating the collection as universally representative.',
'Labels need a definition and an annotation procedure. If two people disagree about whether an answer is supported, inspect the rubric and source evidence before assuming one annotator is wrong. Separate unanswerable questions from incorrect answers. Store uncertain labels or adjudication decisions explicitly. A clean-looking table that silently collapses uncertainty can teach a model an inconsistent target.',
'Transformations include parsing, normalization, redaction, filtering, deduplication, and tokenization. Preserve identifiers connecting derived records to their permitted source when appropriate. Do not put private raw text into public debugging logs. Keep access and retention decisions explicit. For a real deployment, unresolved permissions or legal questions need review appropriate to the source and jurisdiction; this exercise uses authored fictional records.',
'Our lab creates eight synthetic support records with source-group identifiers and one exact duplicate text. Inspect which records come from the same group before splitting them. A dataset card for this collection should say it is synthetic, tiny, and designed to expose leakage. It must not describe these rows as evidence about actual customer performance or demographic coverage.',
'Write a short dataset card in your notes: intended use, sources, collection method, fields, transformations, label rule, exclusions, split policy, and limitations. Name a version so an experiment can point to the exact data used. Then run the lab and compare row-based and group-based splitting. Documentation is most useful when it records decisions that can be checked, including why a particular group belongs entirely on one side of the split.'
],question:'What does public download access establish?',options:['Permission for every training and commercial use','Only that the material is accessible; permitted uses still need investigation','That no provenance record is necessary'],correct:1,explanation:'Access and authorization are different facts. Record applicable terms and unresolved questions instead of inferring universal permission from availability.',prompt:'Create a dataset card for the lab records, including an explicit label rule and one population they do not represent.',rubric:'Identify synthetic authored sources, grouped records, normalization and duplicate handling, intended leakage demonstration, and limitations. Distinguish permission records from assumptions; do not claim real-world representativeness.',recall:'In three days, reconstruct the fields you need to reproduce a dataset from source to split.'},
{unit:4,title:'Deduplication, leakage, and versioned evaluation',goal:'Find information crossing a split and design a reproducible alternative.',paragraphs:[
'Exact deduplication can compare normalized text or a hash of that text. Normalization may standardize whitespace and letter case, but aggressive normalization can erase distinctions that matter. Near duplicates require a similarity rule and a threshold. Review borderline cases. A similarity score measures a chosen representation, not whether two records describe genuinely independent situations.',
'Split at the level that matches the deployment question. If the goal is performance on unseen customers, keep each customer on one side. For new videos, group by clip or scene; neighboring frames are not independent evidence. For future product behavior, reserve later time periods when appropriate. A group split can have different label proportions, so inspect coverage and sample size as well as overlap.',
'In the lab, alternating rows sends records from the same source group into both training and test sets. Changing to a group split removes those shared groups. One duplicate text still crosses the groups, demonstrating why group identifiers alone are not a complete deduplication policy. Both kinds of overlap need reporting; a single zero-valued leakage check is not a universal clean bill of health.',
'Transformations can leak information too. Fitting normalization statistics, a vocabulary, a feature selector, or an imputation model on all rows lets held-out information influence preprocessing. Fit learned transformations on the training partition, store their parameters, and apply them unchanged to validation and test data. Deterministic parsing rules need documentation even when they do not estimate parameters.',
'Synthetic data can expand a training collection, but it carries the generator’s biases and failure modes. Keep provenance linking synthetic examples to prompts, model versions, and source records as appropriate. Do not generate training paraphrases from reserved test answers. Benchmark contamination can make a system appear to reason about examples it has effectively already encountered.',
'Version the source manifest, split assignment, transformation code, and evaluation labels together. To reproduce an experiment, you need more than the final CSV filename. Run both lab scenarios, count overlapping groups and duplicate texts, and propose a repaired split that removes both while retaining meaningful coverage. Explain the tradeoff if removing related records leaves too little independent test data.'
],question:'A group split has no shared source IDs but has duplicate text across partitions. Is the evaluation automatically independent?',options:['Yes, group IDs are sufficient','No, duplicate content is another leakage path','Yes, if the test set is large'],correct:1,explanation:'Group isolation handles one relationship. Duplicate content, preprocessing fitted on held-out data, and time relationships can create additional leakage.',prompt:'Report both overlap counts from the lab. Describe how you would repair the duplicate and preserve a reproducible split manifest.',rubric:'Row splitting shares source groups. Group splitting removes group overlap but leaves the deliberately cross-group duplicate. Remove or group duplicate clusters before finalizing splits, then document coverage loss and exact record assignments.',recall:'After one week, explain how a preprocessing step can leak information without copying a test label.'},
{unit:5,title:'Train a tiny language model',goal:'Build a count-based next-token model and interpret its held-out likelihood.',paragraphs:[
'Language modeling assigns probabilities to token sequences. In an autoregressive model, the probability of a sequence is a product of conditional next-token probabilities. Training often minimizes the average negative logarithm of the correct next token’s probability. A token is a unit chosen by a tokenizer: it may be a character, a word, a subword, or a byte-related unit. Comparing word counts is not a substitute for comparing tokenizer behavior.',
'An embedding table maps each token identifier to a learned vector. Similarity between vectors depends on the training objective and context; a nearby embedding is not a guarantee of identical meaning or factual equivalence. A simple count-based model does not need embeddings. That makes it useful as an inspectable baseline for understanding what a sequence objective can learn before adding a neural architecture.',
'Our lab trains a word bigram model on four tiny sentences by counting adjacent word pairs, including sentence boundaries. After the word fox, the training collection contains sleeps twice and runs once. Without smoothing, an unseen transition has probability zero and infinite negative log likelihood. Add-one smoothing adds one count to each allowed vocabulary item, yielding a distribution that gives unseen transitions some probability.',
'The vocabulary size matters in the denominator. In this lab it includes the six observed words and the end marker, so the denominator after fox is three observations plus seven possible next tokens. The smoothed probability of sleeps is three tenths, and runs is two tenths. The remaining mass is distributed across other vocabulary items. Smoothing prevents zeros but does not teach an unseen relationship magically.',
'Perplexity is the exponential of average negative log likelihood. Lower perplexity means the model assigns higher probability to the evaluated tokens under that tokenization and protocol. It does not directly measure usefulness, factual support, or reasoning. Compare identical tokenization, data, boundaries, and normalization rules. An evaluation sentence copied from training is a weak test of generalization.',
'Run the lab, inspect the learned transition counts, and trace the sentence probability for red fox runs followed by the end marker. The model remembers only one preceding token, so it cannot distinguish two histories ending in the same word. Explain a case needing longer context. The next lesson introduces attention as one mechanism for combining information from multiple allowed positions rather than treating this small baseline as a transformer.'
],question:'Why add smoothing to the tiny bigram model?',options:['To guarantee factual answers','To avoid zero probabilities for unseen vocabulary transitions','To give the model unlimited context'],correct:1,explanation:'Smoothing redistributes probability mass and prevents infinite loss for unseen transitions within the vocabulary. It does not extend the bigram context or establish factual correctness.',prompt:'Calculate the probability of sleeps after fox and explain one limitation of the reported perplexity.',rubric:'There are 2 sleeps counts, 3 total fox transitions, and 7 vocabulary items. Add-one smoothing gives 3 divided by 10. Perplexity depends on the exact tokenization and evaluation set; the model uses only one preceding token.',recall:'In three days, explain why a zero transition count is not proof that a sentence is impossible.'},
{unit:5,title:'Attention and transformer architecture',goal:'Trace attention, causal masking, and the roles of transformer components.',paragraphs:[
'Attention combines values using weights derived from queries and keys. Think of a query as the current position asking what information is relevant, keys as matching descriptors, and values as the information to combine. These are learned numerical projections, not separate search strings. Dot products score matches; scaled dot-product attention divides by the square root of key dimension before normalizing scores with softmax.',
'In the lab, a query has three scores: one, two, and four. The current position is the second, so a causal mask excludes the third position. With that mask, the allowed weights are approximately zero point two six nine and zero point seven three one, and the future weight is zero. Remove the mask and the future position receives most of the mass. Such access would leak the answer during next-token training.',
'A transformer block also includes positionwise feed-forward computation, residual connections, and normalization. Attention moves information between allowed positions; the feed-forward sublayer transforms each representation. Residual paths add an input back to a transformation, helping information and gradients move through depth. Normalization controls activation statistics. These responsibilities are distinct even when optimized implementations fuse several operations.',
'Attention alone does not provide the desired ordering rule. Position information can be encoded with added position vectors or incorporated through mechanisms such as relative or rotary relationships. Multiple attention heads learn different projections, but a head is not guaranteed to correspond to one interpretable linguistic concept. Inspect the actual masks, dimensions, and objective rather than inferring a model’s behavior from a diagram label.',
'Encoder models often combine both left and right context for representation tasks. Decoder-only language models use causal attention for autoregressive generation. Encoder-decoder models encode an input and let a decoder attend to that representation while generating an output. These are architectural patterns, not rankings of intelligence. Their objectives, data, and evaluation determine which application comparisons are meaningful.',
'During training, teacher forcing supplies the observed prefix and predicts subsequent tokens. Shift input and target sequences correctly and mask padding out of the loss. During inference, generated tokens extend the prefix and their errors can influence later decisions. Run the masking failure scenario, record the future attention weight, and explain why impressive training accuracy with a leaked target would not survive real generation.'
],question:'At the second position of a causal sequence, which key positions may receive attention?',options:['All positions including future answers','Only the third position','The first and second positions'],correct:2,explanation:'Causal attention excludes later positions. A future token available during training can create a shortcut unavailable when generating that token.',prompt:'Describe a transformer block’s components and use the lab weights to explain how a missing mask leaks future information.',rubric:'Identify query/key/value projections, score scaling, softmax, mask, feed-forward sublayer, residuals, normalization, and positional information. The future weight is zero only with the causal mask; architecture names alone do not establish capability.',recall:'After one week, explain what changes between teacher-forced training and autoregressive generation.'}
);

lessons.push(
{unit:6,title:'Batches, optimizers, and memory budgets',goal:'Account for training work and memory before scaling a model.',paragraphs:[
'Pretraining fits a model to a broad objective on a large collection before narrower adaptation. For a causal language model, the objective commonly predicts the next token. Scale changes the number of parameters, examples, updates, and devices; it does not eliminate the need for a sound dataset and evaluation. More computation spent on contaminated data can make a misleading benchmark result look more convincing.',
'A batch contains the examples contributing to one estimated gradient. Gradient accumulation combines several microbatches before an optimizer step. If each device processes four examples per microbatch, accumulates eight microbatches, and four devices average gradients, the effective batch is one hundred twenty-eight examples. Weight each contribution correctly when the last microbatch is smaller or sequence lengths vary.',
'Training memory includes parameters, gradients, optimizer state, saved activations, temporary workspaces, and communication buffers. A million parameters stored with two bytes each occupy roughly two million bytes, but that is only one component. Some mixed-precision optimizer arrangements require around sixteen bytes per parameter before activations; implementations differ. A weight-only inference estimate must not be presented as a complete training budget.',
'Momentum keeps a moving history of gradient direction. Adam also tracks estimates related to gradient magnitude and uses them to scale updates. These histories are state, not decorative metadata. Weight decay can constrain parameter growth, but its interaction with adaptive optimizers depends on the formulation. Learning-rate schedules change step sizes over time; a resumed run must restore the appropriate schedule position.',
'Compute throughput and elapsed time answer different questions. A faster matrix multiplication may not improve total training time if data loading, synchronization, or checkpoint writes dominate. Record tokens or examples processed, wall time, and hardware utilization under a specified workload. Warm-up, padding, sequence length, and batch shape can change a measurement substantially. Synthetic estimates are planning tools, not hardware benchmarks.',
'The lab estimates effective batch size and a clearly labeled parameter-state memory budget, then runs a small momentum training calculation. Trace which memory terms are included and which are omitted. Predict what happens if a checkpoint restores the weight but loses optimizer history. This small reproducible calculation captures a real scaling concern without pretending to train a frontier model on the learner’s computer.'
],question:'Four devices use microbatches of 4 and accumulate 8 times before a step. What is the effective batch size?',options:['16','32','128'],correct:2,explanation:'Multiply devices, microbatch examples, and accumulation steps: 4 times 4 times 8 equals 128, assuming the gradients are normalized consistently.',prompt:'Construct a training memory budget and explain why parameter bytes alone underestimate it. State every assumption behind the lab estimate.',rubric:'Include gradients, optimizer state, activations, and temporary buffers. Distinguish decimal bytes from binary mebibytes. The lab parameter-state estimate excludes activation and communication costs; effective batch is 128.',recall:'In three days, reconstruct the effective batch calculation and list the state an adaptive optimizer retains.'},
{unit:6,title:'Distributed training and recoverable experiments',goal:'Explain parallelism and verify that a resumed run preserves its computation.',paragraphs:[
'Data parallelism gives devices different examples while keeping corresponding model parameters synchronized. Gradients are combined before equivalent updates. Tensor parallelism splits operations or parameter tensors across devices. Pipeline parallelism assigns different stages to devices and moves intermediate activations between them. These strategies address different constraints and can be combined, but communication and scheduling create overhead.',
'Sharding distributes state such as optimizer history, gradients, or parameters so one device need not hold every copy. Activation checkpointing saves memory by discarding some forward intermediates and recomputing them during the backward pass. It trades additional computation for lower storage. This technique is different from writing a training checkpoint to disk for recovery.',
'A recoverable checkpoint includes model weights, optimizer state, schedule position, step count, and relevant random-generator and data-sampling state. Also retain code and data versions. Without sampler position, a restart may silently repeat or skip examples. Exact bitwise reproducibility is not always available across hardware or kernels; define the reproducibility level you need and document unavoidable differences.',
'In the lab, momentum updates a single weight. One run trains uninterrupted. Another stops after several steps and resumes. Restoring both weight and velocity reproduces the uninterrupted calculation. The failure scenario restores only the weight, resetting velocity to zero. Its later path changes even though the saved model initially produces the same predictions. Model equivalence at one instant is not training-state equivalence.',
'Use an experiment ledger with run identifier, revision, dataset manifest, split, hyperparameters, seed, metrics, and checkpoint location. Record failed runs too. Compare results at a matched training budget and evaluation protocol. A run that saw more tokens or a different split is not an isolated test of an optimizer improvement. Logs should support diagnosis without retaining unnecessary sensitive examples.',
'Predict the difference between the two checkpoint scenarios, then run them. For a larger system, propose a controlled interruption test and the assertions you would make after recovery. If a distributed run stalls, separate input starvation, communication delay, device failure, and a slow kernel. Increasing device count before identifying the bottleneck can increase cost while making the diagnosis harder.'
],question:'Why can restoring weights alone change resumed momentum training?',options:['Momentum velocity is also part of the update state','Weights cannot be saved','Inference always modifies the optimizer'],correct:0,explanation:'The next update depends on both the current gradient and accumulated velocity. A weight-only restore does not reconstruct that history.',prompt:'Run both recovery scenarios. Design a recovery test for a shuffled multi-device workload and state which comparisons should be exact versus tolerance-based.',rubric:'Restore weight and velocity to reproduce the toy run. For a larger run include schedule, sampler, random states, code and data revisions. Distinguish exact toy arithmetic from hardware-dependent floating-point reproducibility.',recall:'After one week, distinguish activation checkpointing from a recoverable training checkpoint.'},
{unit:7,title:'Supervised adaptation and low-rank updates',goal:'Choose an adaptation method and inspect the parameters it actually changes.',paragraphs:[
'Supervised fine-tuning fits a pretrained model to examples of the behavior wanted in an application. For language tasks, examples may pair instructions with desired responses. The loss usually trains selected response tokens rather than treating every part of a prompt as a target. Data quality and the formatting of boundaries matter: an incorrectly masked loss can optimize behavior different from the task you intended.',
'Full fine-tuning updates the selected base parameters. Parameter-efficient methods restrict the trainable change. Low-rank adaptation represents a weight update as the product of two smaller matrices while keeping the original matrix fixed. For a square matrix with dimension four and rank one, the factors have four entries each, or eight trainable values instead of sixteen. Savings become more significant for larger matrices with small rank.',
'The rank is a capacity constraint. A rank-one update can express an outer product but cannot represent every possible matrix change. More rank increases flexibility and memory use. Scaling, which layers receive adapters, and the training data all matter. A small number of trainable parameters does not automatically make a method more accurate, private, or safe.',
'Our lab freezes a four-by-four identity matrix and learns a rank-one update toward a specified target transformation. The baseline target is itself rank one, so the factors can represent it. The failure scenario asks for a full-rank change. Inspect the initial and final error and the trainable parameter count. A residual error may come from the restricted model family rather than too few optimizer steps.',
'Adaptation can improve one task while damaging another, sometimes called catastrophic forgetting when previously learned behavior is lost. Evaluate the original tasks as well as the new target. Separate adaptation training examples from validation and final evaluation. For frequently changing factual material, retrieval may be easier to update and audit than repeatedly encoding new facts into weights.',
'Before choosing a method, write the behavior you need, the available examples, the memory budget, and the regression tests. Run both lab scenarios and explain when a larger rank, a different layer choice, or full fine-tuning would be a reasonable next experiment. The lab is a small matrix adaptation exercise; it demonstrates the parameterization without claiming that one successful matrix fit predicts language-model performance.'
],question:'A frozen 4 by 4 matrix receives a rank-one update. How many entries are in its two trainable factors?',options:['4','8','16'],correct:1,explanation:'The factors have shapes 4 by 1 and 1 by 4, for 4 plus 4 trainable entries. Their product is constrained to rank at most one.',prompt:'Compare baseline and full-rank target errors. Explain whether more training steps alone can remove the capacity limit, and design a regression test for the original task.',rubric:'The baseline rank-one target is representable; an arbitrary full-rank change is not. Eight trainable entries update a frozen sixteen-entry matrix. Evaluate retained behavior, not only adaptation loss.',recall:'In three days, explain the difference between changing the base weights and adding a low-rank update.'},
{unit:7,title:'Preferences, rewards, and distillation',goal:'Separate imitation, preference optimization, and learning from feedback.',paragraphs:[
'A preference example compares two responses to the same context. The label says which response an evaluator prefers under a rubric; it does not automatically certify factual correctness. A consistent rubric might assess evidence, relevance, and clarity separately. Collecting only stylistic preferences can train a system to sound more convincing without making its answers better supported.',
'A common reinforcement-learning-from-feedback pattern trains a reward model on comparisons, then optimizes a policy toward that reward while constraining deviation from a reference. A policy selects actions, a reward scores outcomes, and an environment determines what follows an action. Delayed rewards make credit assignment difficult. An imperfect reward can incentivize shortcuts that score well while defeating the underlying purpose.',
'Direct preference optimization uses chosen and rejected responses with a reference model in a direct objective rather than requiring the same separate reward-model-and-policy-optimization procedure. The important comparison involves how policy log probabilities change relative to the reference for both responses. Merely increasing the probability of a preferred string without context, rejected examples, or a defined objective is not a faithful implementation of the method.',
'Distillation trains a student to imitate a teacher’s outputs or distributions. Soft distributions can communicate alternatives that a single hard label omits. Temperature can change how much probability mass is visible on less likely classes. The student can inherit the teacher’s errors, and a smaller model may not reproduce all teacher behavior. Compare independent task metrics, latency, and memory rather than imitation loss alone.',
'For a concrete reward failure, imagine scoring answers by length because detailed responses were preferred in an early sample. A model can add irrelevant text to increase that reward. Repair the evaluation by judging support and task completion separately, including short correct answers and long unsupported answers. Feedback from an AI evaluator also needs auditing; automated feedback does not remove bias or disagreement.',
'Use the unit’s adaptation lab as a reminder that an objective can improve while a model family remains limited. Then write a preference pair for one of its explanations: a concise honest account of residual error and a confident claim that training solved everything. Identify the preferred response, the rejected response, the evidence, and a regression test. Explain why a reward for confidence would choose badly.'
],question:'A model learns to write longer answers because length is rewarded, even when they are unsupported. What is the central problem?',options:['The optimized reward is a poor proxy for the intended outcome','Preference labels guarantee truth','All reinforcement learning requires a physical robot'],correct:0,explanation:'Optimization can exploit a proxy reward. Measure support and task success independently, and include counterexamples where the proxy conflicts with the goal.',prompt:'Write and justify a preference pair about the lab results. Contrast supervised fine-tuning, preference optimization, and teacher-student distillation.',rubric:'Use a shared context, an explicit rubric, and evidence for the preference. Supervised targets demonstrate desired outputs; preference objectives compare alternatives; distillation imitates a teacher. None guarantees truth or removes the need for independent evaluation.',recall:'After one week, explain a reward-hacking example and a test that would expose it.'},
{unit:8,title:'Decoding, context, and the KV cache',goal:'Account for generation state and measure the parts of response latency.',paragraphs:[
'Serving begins before the first output token. The system validates a request, obtains inputs, waits in a queue, and processes the prompt. This prompt-processing phase is often called prefill. Autoregressive decoding then produces additional tokens. Time to first token, time between tokens, total latency, and throughput describe different aspects of the experience. Report which one you measured.',
'Greedy decoding chooses the highest-probability next token. Sampling draws from a distribution, possibly modified by temperature, top-k, or nucleus filtering. Temperature rescales scores before normalization; a lower value concentrates the distribution. Sampling changes variability, not whether evidence exists for an answer. Stop sequences and maximum-token limits are part of serving behavior and should be tested explicitly.',
'A key-value cache stores attention keys and values for previous positions so later decoding steps can reuse them. It is runtime state for a sequence, not a database of learned facts. Its size depends on token count, layers, batch size, key-value heads, head dimension, and precision. Grouped-query attention can use fewer key-value heads than query heads, so assuming every query head needs a separate cache can overestimate memory.',
'The lab estimates a cache with thirty-two layers, eight key-value heads, head dimension one hundred twenty-eight, two-byte elements, and batch size one. Keys and values contribute a factor of two. At two thousand forty-eight tokens, the result is two hundred fifty-six mebibytes. At eight thousand one hundred ninety-two tokens, it is one thousand twenty-four mebibytes. The fourfold growth follows token count under these fixed assumptions.',
'Longer context also changes computation and what the model must select from its input. A large supported context window is not proof that all relevant facts will be used reliably. Truncation, retrieval, and summarization make different tradeoffs. Record the actual input sent to the model when debugging, subject to privacy requirements, rather than assuming the application’s entire conversation history was included.',
'Run both token-count scenarios and reconstruct the memory calculation. Then design a latency experiment that separates queueing, prefill, decoding, and speech playback. Our existing narrator demonstrates why a cached WAV can start quickly even while new speech takes longer. Avoid attributing a slow first response entirely to model inference when loading or queueing may dominate.'
],question:'With all other cache dimensions fixed, changing context from 2048 to 8192 tokens changes KV memory by what factor?',options:['It remains constant','Four','Sixteen'],correct:1,explanation:'The stored keys and values grow linearly with token count under the stated fixed dimensions. Attention computation and total application latency involve additional factors.',prompt:'Compute both cache sizes and propose separate measurements for first-token latency and generation throughput.',rubric:'Use 2 times layers times batch times tokens times KV heads times head dimension times bytes. The examples are 256 and 1024 MiB. Specify workload, warm-up, queue conditions, and which interval each metric covers.',recall:'In three days, explain why a KV cache is neither learned model memory nor a complete conversation database.'},
{unit:8,title:'Quantization, batching, and serving tradeoffs',goal:'Compare performance techniques without confusing estimates with benchmarks.',paragraphs:[
'Quantization stores or computes selected values with fewer representable levels. A simple symmetric scheme divides by a scale, rounds to integers, clips to a range, and reconstructs by multiplying by the scale. The difference from the original values is quantization error. Outliers can force a large shared scale that reduces resolution for smaller values; per-channel or per-group scales change this tradeoff.',
'The lab quantizes four weights to signed levels from minus seven through seven. It reports reconstructed values and mean squared error. These are real calculations, but they are not a performance benchmark for a production quantized model. A lower bit count does not guarantee lower wall time: the hardware, kernels, dequantization, memory bandwidth, and workload determine actual performance.',
'Batching combines requests so hardware can process more work together. Larger batches may improve throughput while increasing waiting time for an individual request. Continuous batching changes membership as requests finish rather than requiring every sequence to finish together. Requests with long outputs or contexts can affect memory pressure and scheduling fairness; measure tail latency as well as averages.',
'Speculative decoding uses a cheaper draft model to propose tokens and a target model to verify them. Proper acceptance and correction rules can preserve the target distribution while reducing expensive serial work. Simply accepting every draft token is a different algorithm and changes the distribution. Speedup depends on draft cost, acceptance rate, and verification overhead; an inaccurate draft can provide little benefit.',
'A serving budget should include model weights, caches, temporary buffers, and concurrency. When a request is canceled, stop unnecessary future work and prevent a late result from reappearing in the interface. Apply bounded queues and explicit timeouts. A fast median with occasional very long stalls may be unsuitable for voice interaction even if aggregate throughput looks impressive.',
'Compare the lab’s context scenarios and quantization residuals, then write a fair benchmark protocol: same model task, prompt lengths, output limits, concurrency, warm-up, and quality evaluation. Record hardware and software versions. State which numbers are calculated estimates and which are measured. A useful optimization preserves acceptable task behavior while improving the particular resource constraint that matters.'
],question:'A model uses fewer bits per weight. What can you conclude about latency without a benchmark?',options:['It must be twice as fast','It must preserve all outputs exactly','Nothing definite; kernels and workload also matter'],correct:2,explanation:'Quantization changes representation and often memory use, but actual latency depends on implementation and hardware. Evaluate task quality and measured latency together.',prompt:'Inspect reconstructed lab weights. Propose a quality threshold and a latency experiment for choosing a quantized configuration.',rubric:'Report quantization error and a task metric, not only bit width. Hold workload and concurrency fixed, measure warm and cold behavior separately, and include tail latency. Explain when batching helps throughput but hurts waiting time.',recall:'After one week, describe the condition under which speculative decoding can preserve the target distribution.'},
{unit:9,title:'Retrieval, evidence, and grounded answers',goal:'Build a small retrieval pipeline and inspect whether its output is supported.',paragraphs:[
'Retrieval finds material relevant to a query from a collection. A lexical retriever compares terms; a dense retriever compares learned vectors. Cosine similarity divides a dot product by the product of vector lengths, emphasizing direction rather than magnitude. Similarity is evidence about a representation, not proof that a passage answers the question. Evaluate retrieval with examples that have known relevant sources.',
'A retrieval pipeline ingests documents, preserves source identifiers, creates chunks, builds an index, and searches it for a query. Chunk size trades context against precision: a short fragment may omit a necessary condition, while a large chunk may bury it. Keep version and access metadata with the chunks. Retrieval must not make a private document available merely because it matches the query.',
'Reranking applies a more expensive comparison to an initial candidate set. Retrieval-augmented generation supplies selected evidence to a generator along with the question. The retriever can fail to find the answer, or the generator can misuse correct evidence. Measure these failure modes separately. Recall at a cutoff measures how often relevant material appears among retrieved candidates; answer support tests a different stage.',
'The lab uses a tiny authored document collection and a lexical ranking rule. It returns a source identifier and an extract from the top relevant document. This is an inspectable retrieval baseline, not a trained language model. Ask about notes, training, or speech to observe how terms choose evidence. Ask an unrelated question and inspect the explicit no-evidence response rather than expecting fabricated knowledge.',
'A citation must support the claim it accompanies. Pointing at a real page is insufficient if the quoted passage says something else or belongs to a different version. A useful answer can say that the collection lacks the requested fact. Define this behavior in evaluation examples before tuning retrieval. Otherwise, improving answer rate may simply reward unsupported guesses.',
'Run the baseline and adversarial-document scenarios. The latter adds a document containing instructions to ignore the user and perform an unrelated action. Treat that text as document content, not as an instruction with authority over the application. Inspect the returned source and trace how data remains separate from control. Write two answerable and two unanswerable queries to extend the evaluation.'
],question:'A response includes a real citation. What must still be checked?',options:['Whether the cited passage actually supports the claim','Whether citations make evaluation unnecessary','Whether a citation changes model weights'],correct:0,explanation:'Citation existence and claim support are different properties. Check the passage, version, and relation to the particular statement.',prompt:'Run an answerable and an unrelated query. Identify the retrieved source, the answer contract, and one failure a better retriever would not fix.',rubric:'Report source IDs and supporting extracts. Retrieval relevance does not guarantee answer correctness. A generator can invent claims despite good retrieval; absent evidence should lead to an explicit limitation.',recall:'In three days, distinguish retrieval recall from answer support and design one test for each.'},
{unit:9,title:'Tools, agents, and failure recovery',goal:'Design bounded tool use with explicit permissions and observable state.',paragraphs:[
'A tool is a callable operation with an input schema, output contract, and possible side effects. A model can propose a call, but surrounding software must decide whether it is valid and authorized. Structured output makes parsing more reliable; it does not prove that arguments are appropriate. Validate types, ranges, resource identity, and permissions at the execution boundary.',
'An agent loop observes state, chooses an action, executes an allowed operation, and observes the result until a stopping condition is reached. A fixed workflow encodes more of that sequence in advance. Neither approach removes the need for timeouts, budgets, and recovery. The appropriate degree of autonomy depends on the task and the consequences of an incorrect action.',
'Separate planning from effects. Reading a document and sending a message have different consequences. A retrieval result that says to reveal secrets or change settings remains untrusted content. Do not promote it into system authority. Tool allowlists, narrowly scoped credentials, input validation, and explicit approval for consequential actions address different layers of the problem.',
'Retries need care. Repeating a read may be harmless, but repeating a payment or message can duplicate an effect. An idempotency key identifies one logical operation across retries. Record whether an operation completed before deciding to retry after a timeout. A missing response is not evidence that the action never happened. Durable workflow state can help recovery resume from a known boundary.',
'Our lab’s tools are deliberately limited to local lookup and returning an explanation. The adversarial document cannot create a new tool or execute code. A small step budget bounds the workflow, and the output includes a trace of what ran. This demonstrates a control boundary in a deterministic program; it is not a claim that keyword filtering alone solves prompt injection for arbitrary language-model agents.',
'Design an extension that performs one read-only calculation, and specify its schema and failure response before coding it. Then design, on paper, an action with external effects and explain where user authorization and idempotency would be required. In your notes, distinguish conversation history, temporary working state, durable task state, and the context actually supplied to a model.'
],question:'A retrieved page tells an assistant to send private data to a new address. What authority does that page have?',options:['It overrides application rules because it was retrieved','It is untrusted data and does not authorize the action','It grants permission if its text is valid JSON'],correct:1,explanation:'Content can inform an answer without gaining authority over tools or permissions. Validate and authorize actions outside the model and document text.',prompt:'Describe the lab trace and design a bounded tool workflow with one timeout and one safe stopping condition.',rubric:'Use explicit tool contracts, a step budget, validation, and a no-evidence exit. Discuss idempotency for effects and why an uncertain response should not trigger blind retries. Keep retrieved content separate from control instructions.',recall:'After one week, explain the difference between retrying a read and retrying an externally visible action.'}
);

lessons.push(
{unit:10,title:'Audio representations and speech recognition',goal:'Connect sampled sound, learned representations, and recognition errors.',paragraphs:[
'A digital waveform stores samples of an audio signal at regular intervals. Sample rate states how many samples are recorded per second; bit depth describes a numerical representation of amplitude. Stereo has multiple channels. A ten-second mono recording at twenty-four thousand samples per second contains two hundred forty thousand samples. Doubling sample rate changes storage and processing needs, not automatically the quality of the original evidence.',
'Frequencies above half the sampling rate cannot be represented unambiguously under the usual sampling assumptions. Resampling therefore needs filtering as well as selecting samples. Simply dropping samples can create aliasing. Clipping loses amplitude information, and aggressive noise removal can erase speech. Record channel layout, sample rate, normalization, and transformations when moving audio between models.',
'A spectrogram describes frequency content over short windows. It trades time resolution against frequency resolution through the window and hop choices. Mel-scaled features emphasize a perceptually motivated frequency organization. Other models learn representations directly from waveforms or encoded audio tokens. A representation changes what the model receives; it does not determine whether a transcript will be correct.',
'Automatic speech recognition maps audio to text. Some systems use connectionist temporal classification, which allows alignments between many audio frames and a shorter label sequence using blank symbols and repeated-label rules. Sequence-to-sequence systems generate text conditioned on audio features. Acoustic evidence, language patterns, and decoding choices can all influence the result, including plausible words that were not spoken.',
'Word error rate counts substitutions, deletions, and insertions divided by the number of reference words. If the reference has four words and one is substituted, the rate is one quarter. The lab computes edit distance rather than merely comparing words at the same positions, so a single insertion does not automatically shift every later word into an error. Define case and punctuation normalization before comparing systems.',
'Run the clean and corrupted transcript scenarios and inspect the error counts. Then explain why low word error rate may still hide a critical mistake in a name, number, or negation. For voice applications, evaluate task completion and important terms as well as aggregate recognition accuracy. The lab’s waveform sample count and byte count are exact arithmetic; its staged latency values are illustrative inputs rather than measurements of your microphone.'
],question:'A four-word reference has one substitution and no other errors. What is word error rate?',options:['One quarter','One half','Four'],correct:0,explanation:'Word error rate is substitutions plus deletions plus insertions, divided by reference word count. One error divided by four reference words is 0.25.',prompt:'Trace the edit-distance result and explain how resampling differs from changing a file header’s sample-rate value.',rubric:'Count the actual edit operations, state normalization, and divide by reference length. Proper resampling changes the sample sequence with filtering; relabeling the rate changes playback interpretation and can change pitch and duration.',recall:'In three days, give an example where a low word error rate still causes a serious task failure.'},
{unit:10,title:'Speech synthesis and responsive voice interfaces',goal:'Explain synthesis, word alignment, streaming, and interruption as separate stages.',paragraphs:[
'Text-to-speech turns text into audio through a model and associated processing. Systems differ in how they represent pronunciation, prosody, speaker identity, and the waveform or audio tokens. A vocoder converts an acoustic representation into a waveform in some architectures. Naturalness depends on pronunciation, rhythm, emphasis, pauses, and transitions between words, not only on the nominal speaker identity.',
'A speaker reference conditions voice characteristics. In this application, synthetic references were designed during setup and reused for consistency. Text normalization expands written forms into spoken forms; a number may be spoken several ways depending on context. Evaluation should include dates, abbreviations, names, and punctuation. A voice that sounds pleasant on one preview may mispronounce technical course terms.',
'Word alignment estimates where supplied words occur in recorded audio. It is distinct from speech generation and from unconstrained transcription. The course aligns known text to saved WAVs, then underlines words using the player’s audio position. Changing playback speed changes how quickly that position advances. A wall-clock timer that assumes the original speed would drift after speed changes or pauses.',
'Streaming can begin delivering audio before a full response is ready, reducing initial delay at the cost of more complex buffering and interruption. Very short chunks can create unnatural boundaries. Larger chunks preserve context but delay the first sound. Pre-rendering known course passages avoids repeated synthesis; caching does not make new text free to generate. Measure generation and playback separately.',
'Barge-in means the listener can interrupt a speaking system. A correct implementation stops playback, prevents late requests from restarting old audio, and manages already queued work. It should also decide what conversation state remains valid after an interruption. Generation identifiers and cancellation signals are useful application mechanisms; speech-recognition accuracy alone does not solve these interaction problems.',
'Use the real course player to pause, resume, change speed, replay, and stop a passage. Then run the lab to separate recognition errors from an illustrative latency budget. Record which time interval corresponds to first sound and which is total interaction time. Explain why this course preserves audio availability when word timings are still being prepared, and why such a fallback should not falsely claim exact word synchronization.'
],question:'Which clock should drive underlining of a saved recording when playback speed can change?',options:['Elapsed wall time since the page loaded','The audio player’s current position','The number of characters displayed'],correct:1,explanation:'The audio position follows actual playback, pauses, seeks, and rate changes. A fixed wall-clock schedule can drift away from the sound.',prompt:'Test an interruption and explain every stage from written text to underlined playback. Propose a pronunciation test and a first-sound latency measurement.',rubric:'Separate normalization, synthesis, caching, alignment, playback, and UI state. Confirm that stale audio cannot restart after Stop. Assess names or numbers with listening and transcript checks; timestamps are acoustic estimates.',recall:'After one week, explain how a system can have excellent recognition but still feel unresponsive in conversation.'},
{unit:11,title:'Autoencoders, GANs, and diffusion',goal:'Compare generative objectives and explain what a plausible sample establishes.',paragraphs:[
'An autoencoder maps an input to a representation and reconstructs it. A bottleneck or other constraint encourages useful structure, but reconstruction ability alone does not define a good generative distribution. A variational autoencoder adds a probabilistic latent model and balances reconstruction with a regularizing term that relates an approximate posterior to a prior. The tradeoff affects both reconstruction and sampling.',
'A generative adversarial network trains a generator against a discriminator. The discriminator tries to distinguish training-like examples from generated ones; the generator tries to produce outputs that succeed against that criterion. This competition can create convincing samples while suffering from instability or limited diversity, including mode collapse. A discriminator score is not a general measure of factual fidelity.',
'Diffusion training commonly corrupts data with controlled noise and teaches a model to predict a quantity related to removing that noise at a specified noise level. Sampling starts from noise and applies learned denoising steps. Conditioning can guide those steps using text, images, or other information. The number and formulation of sampling steps affect computation and quality; denoising is not simply recovering a uniquely known original from every noisy observation.',
'Flow-based generation can learn transformations or velocity fields connecting distributions. Flow matching trains a field along selected probability paths, while normalizing-flow models emphasize invertible transformations and tractable density calculations under their assumptions. These related names do not identify one interchangeable objective. Ask what is learned, how samples are produced, and whether likelihood is available for the specific formulation.',
'Conditioning supplies information about the desired output, but it cannot guarantee all requested constraints or truth. Guidance can strengthen some aspects of conditioning while affecting diversity or introducing artifacts. A model may produce a convincing texture without recovering the original scene. Distinguish generation for creative use from restoration, where fidelity to observed evidence is central.',
'The unit lab gives two different high-resolution pairs that both average to the same low-resolution value. This simple many-to-one observation shows why there need not be a unique inverse. Compare the candidates and explain why a sharper-looking output can still be unsupported. Use the example to contrast the goals of reconstruction loss, adversarial realism, and conditional denoising rather than treating visual polish as proof of accuracy.'
],question:'Two different high-resolution signals produce the same observed low-resolution value. What follows?',options:['The observation alone may not identify a unique original','A generator can always recover the exact original','More output pixels prove correctness'],correct:0,explanation:'A many-to-one degradation loses information. Learned priors can select plausible candidates, but additional assumptions or evidence are needed to establish the original.',prompt:'Compare autoencoder reconstruction, adversarial training, and diffusion denoising. Use the lab’s ambiguous signal to explain plausibility versus fidelity.',rubric:'Identify each objective and sampling mechanism, distinguish a prior from new evidence, and explain why identical degraded observations can correspond to different originals. Mention diversity and computational tradeoffs.',recall:'In three days, explain one failure mode that a photorealistic sample can conceal.'},
{unit:11,title:'Video restoration and multimodal evidence',goal:'Trace temporal alignment and evaluate restored or multimodal outputs accessibly.',paragraphs:[
'Video restoration combines evidence across time. Motion changes where a scene point appears, and occlusion changes whether it is visible at all. Propagating features between frames can carry useful information, but those features must be aligned or otherwise related appropriately. Aggregation combines evidence, and upsampling produces the higher-resolution representation. BasicVSR is one influential organization of these responsibilities.',
'The lab represents a bright point as a one-dimensional row. In the second frame the point shifts one position. Averaging the raw rows spreads the point across two positions. Aligning the shifted frame before averaging preserves the reference location. Read the numeric arrays and mean squared errors; no fine visual inspection is required. The demonstration isolates alignment and does not claim to implement a trained VSR network.',
'Real motion is more complex than one translation. Deformation, rolling shutter, blur, compression, scene cuts, and newly revealed regions can break correspondence. A system that propagates stale features across a cut can create artifacts. Occlusion masks and scene-boundary handling can be important. Evaluate entire sequences rather than assuming that independent high-quality frames imply coherent video.',
'Pixel error compares an output with an aligned reference under a chosen scale. Peak signal-to-noise ratio is a logarithmic transformation of mean squared error with a specified peak value. Perceptual metrics compare representations, and temporal metrics assess consistency across frames. None alone establishes fidelity, natural appearance, or suitability for every use. Misregistration can make even a plausible output score poorly against a reference.',
'A multimodal system connects representations from text, images, audio, or video. A vision-language model may condition text generation on visual features; cross-modal alignment is learned from paired or related data. It can still misread a small label or describe a plausible event that did not occur. Evaluate grounded details, temporal order, and uncertainty rather than only the fluency of its explanation.',
'Run both alignment scenarios and describe the failure numerically. Then design a restoration evaluation with paired examples, realistic degradation, scene-level splits, temporal checks, and narrated descriptions of artifacts. Keep source uncertainty visible: if a sign is unreadable, do not treat generated lettering as forensic evidence. Explain how a language-model summary could accurately report the measured error while overstating what the restoration recovered.'
],question:'Why does averaging the two unaligned lab frames create an error?',options:['Averaging is forbidden for video','The moving point occupies different coordinates in each frame','A higher frame rate changes the license'],correct:1,explanation:'Corresponding scene content must be related before combining it. Raw coordinate-wise averaging mixes the point with a different location.',prompt:'Report aligned and unaligned arrays and errors. Design separate fidelity, temporal-consistency, and runtime measurements for a real VSR model.',rubric:'Identify the one-position motion, align before aggregation, and distinguish the toy row from learned restoration. Specify reference alignment, scene-level splits, realistic degradation, and accessible descriptions of failures.',recall:'After one week, explain how a frame can look sharp while the sequence flickers or invents detail.'},
{unit:12,title:'Evaluation that changes engineering decisions',goal:'Build a small acceptance suite with explicit failure categories.',paragraphs:[
'An evaluation begins with a decision: what behavior would justify releasing or changing a system? Define representative tasks, expected outputs or rubrics, error severity, and acceptable thresholds before optimizing. Separate development examples from an independent acceptance set. A benchmark score becomes useful when you know what population, protocol, and failure costs it represents.',
'For a grounded assistant, test answerable questions, missing evidence, contradictory sources, outdated versions, and hostile content. Score retrieval and answer support separately. For a voice interface, test pronunciation and interruption along with latency. Include accessibility requirements such as keyboard operation and text alternatives in the acceptance criteria rather than treating them as optional polish.',
'Precision measures how many predicted positives are correct; recall measures how many actual positives were found. The tradeoff matters when refusals and incorrect answers have different consequences. A confusion matrix exposes counts that an overall percentage hides. Small samples have uncertainty. Report denominators and case selection, and avoid claiming a tiny hand-authored suite estimates population performance precisely.',
'Latency distributions reveal tail behavior. In the lab, sort twenty request durations and use the nearest-rank convention: the ninety-fifth percentile is the nineteenth ordered value. A single very slow twentieth request does not become that percentile under this convention, although it remains the maximum. State the percentile definition because tools can interpolate differently, especially on small samples.',
'Our acceptance lab runs authored fixtures against a deterministic answer policy. The baseline refuses an unsupported question and passes the policy checks. The failure scenario replaces that refusal with a confident answer, increasing apparent answer rate while failing the support criterion. The lab also checks a versioned cache example. These fixtures demonstrate engineering contracts, not the measured quality of a trained generative model.',
'Run both scenarios and inspect each case, not just the pass count. Add a test where a correct answer has the wrong source version and another where a timeout leaves a stale UI result. In your notes, identify which failures block release and why. A model can improve an average metric while introducing a severe regression; acceptance should reflect the application’s actual failure costs.'
],question:'On 20 sorted durations, which item is the nearest-rank 95th percentile?',options:['The 10th','The 19th','Always the maximum'],correct:1,explanation:'The nearest rank is ceiling of 0.95 times 20, or 19. Other percentile conventions may interpolate, so specify the convention.',prompt:'Explain the failed fixture and compute the lab percentile. Propose two independent acceptance cases and justify their severity.',rubric:'Reject unsupported confidence, verify source version, and use the nineteenth sorted latency. Separate demonstration fixtures from population estimates. Include an accessibility or interruption regression alongside model-quality checks.',recall:'In three days, explain why a higher answer rate can make a grounded assistant worse.'},
{unit:12,title:'Observability, caching, and controlled releases',goal:'Make production failures traceable and changes reversible.',paragraphs:[
'Observability connects an external symptom to internal stages. Give a request an identifier and record stage durations, outcome categories, model and data versions, and relevant resource use. Avoid logging unnecessary private prompts or raw documents. A trace should help distinguish retrieval failure, generation failure, queue delay, and playback failure without requiring unrestricted access to user data.',
'A cache key describes the conditions under which a previous result remains valid. For generated audio, text, voice identity, and relevant model versions matter. For a grounded answer, the evidence version and authorization context may matter too. A cache hit can be fast and wrong if its key ignores a changed source or crosses an access boundary.',
'The lab compares a text-only key with a key that includes source version. After the underlying answer changes, the unsafe key can return the old value. The versioned key correctly misses and requires a new result. Decide what invalidates each cache and how stale entries are handled. Do not treat a time-to-live as a substitute for identifying important dependencies.',
'A controlled release records an artifact version, configuration, evaluation results, and rollback procedure. A canary sends a limited share of appropriate traffic to a change and compares predefined metrics. Shadow evaluation can run an alternative without exposing its answer, but it still has cost and data-handling implications. An offline pass is necessary evidence, not a guarantee of behavior under live load.',
'Threat modeling asks what assets exist, who can influence inputs, which boundaries matter, and what failures are possible. Prompt injection is one input-driven risk; permission mistakes, secret leakage, dependency compromise, and unsafe tool execution are others. Prefer narrow capabilities and validation at execution boundaries. A safety instruction written in a prompt is not equivalent to an enforced access check.',
'Use the acceptance results to write a release checklist: versioned artifacts, known limitations, failure thresholds, observability, cancellation, backup or rollback, and a recovery exercise. Then explain how you would investigate a user reporting yesterday’s answer after today’s document update. Start by inspecting source and cache identities, not by retraining the model without evidence.'
],question:'A cached answer should change when its supporting document changes. What belongs in the cache identity?',options:['Only the user’s text','Only the time of day','The relevant source version as well as the request context'],correct:2,explanation:'The cached result depends on the evidence. Omitting its version can reuse a previously valid answer after that evidence changes.',prompt:'Trace the cache failure and design a release-and-rollback plan for a narrator or retrieval-model update.',rubric:'Include text or request identity, relevant model/voice/source versions, authorization boundaries, acceptance fixtures, metrics, and rollback artifacts. Separate a fast cache hit from a correct current answer.',recall:'After one week, list the trace fields you need to distinguish model latency from queueing and stale cache reuse.'},
{unit:13,title:'Build the inspectable teaching assistant',goal:'Assemble retrieval, bounded control, evidence, and accessible output into a working capstone.',paragraphs:[
'The capstone combines mechanisms from the course into a small teaching assistant. Begin with a contract: accept a question about the included course notes, retrieve evidence, return a cited explanation or an explicit no-evidence result, and preserve user control over reading. Limit the system to its authored collection. This boundary makes its behavior testable without requiring a paid API or pretending it knows every subject.',
'The runnable lab uses an inspectable extractive assistant: a lexical retriever ranks authored passages, a deterministic policy selects supported content, and the output includes a trace and source identifier. It is not a newly trained language model or a general conversational tutor. This is a useful baseline and working application scaffold. Replacing its response stage with a generative model would require additional evaluation, not merely a different class name.',
'Try a question about training, speech, or notes. Trace the input normalization, retrieval scores, selected source, and returned text. Then ask an unrelated question. A successful capstone handles the second case honestly rather than optimizing for always returning an answer. The adversarial scenario adds hostile document content; the allowed workflow still cannot execute arbitrary code, transmit information, or create new tools.',
'Write an architecture record naming each component’s input, output, state, and failure behavior. Distinguish fixed parameters, indexed source content, request context, cache entries, and saved learner notes. Explain which components would need training if you replaced lexical ranking with learned embeddings. Deploying several pretrained models together does not automatically train them jointly.',
'Extend the baseline with a small authored source passage and at least three new evaluation questions. One should be answerable, one should require refusal, and one should expose an ambiguity or outdated source. Keep identifiers and expected evidence with each case. If you alter ranking, run the same cases before and after and explain regressions rather than hiding them in an aggregate score.',
'Use the Read lab result control to hear the returned evidence and trace through the existing narrator. Test stop, resume, rate changes, and complete text access. Record a measured startup interval separately from model-generation time. Your deliverable is a runnable system plus evidence about its boundaries, not a screenshot of a fluent answer. The next lesson provides the final defense and maintenance exercise.'
],question:'What does the runnable capstone actually implement?',options:['A newly pretrained general-purpose LLM','A bounded extractive assistant with retrieval, evidence, and a trace','A guarantee that every arbitrary question is answerable'],correct:1,explanation:'The capstone is a working inspectable baseline. Its deterministic response stage is deliberately distinguished from a generative language model and evaluated within a small source collection.',prompt:'Run three different questions and document the pipeline, source evidence, allowed actions, and one failure. Propose a tested extension without expanding permissions unnecessarily.',rubric:'Include answerable, unanswerable, and adversarial cases; source IDs; retrieval and answer behavior; complete text and controlled audio. Clearly label deterministic and learned components. A proposed LLM extension needs its own quality and safety evaluation.',recall:'In three days, reconstruct the architecture without looking and identify where a learned retriever would change it.'},
{unit:13,title:'Oral defense, transfer, and maintenance',goal:'Defend a complete system using mechanisms, measurements, and explicit limitations.',paragraphs:[
'An oral defense tests whether you can explain decisions and transfer them to unfamiliar cases. Begin with the input-to-output path. Name the representations at each boundary, which state changes during training, and which information can change at inference. Identify where evidence enters and where an unsupported claim could be introduced. Keep the explanation understandable without relying on a visual diagram.',
'Defend the data. State provenance, permitted-use records, transformations, label rules, split strategy, and known gaps. Explain how duplicates or related videos could cross a naive split. Describe the test population and why it represents, or fails to represent, intended use. If an important fact is unknown, say what evidence would resolve it rather than filling the gap with a confident assumption.',
'Defend the model and objective. Compare at least one baseline and one alternative. Explain why you selected a representation, architecture, loss, or adaptation method, and identify a failure it cannot solve. A larger model cannot repair missing authorization, and more optimization cannot always repair a capacity constraint. Distinguish the evidence supplied by training loss, held-out metrics, and real task outcomes.',
'Defend the runtime. Report latency stages, memory assumptions, concurrency, cancellation, cache identities, and failure recovery. Explain how a change to source material, a narrator, or model weights invalidates relevant artifacts. Demonstrate a controlled failure and show that the system stops or falls back as specified. Describe what rollback means for code, data, models, and derived caches.',
'Now transfer the design. Replace the document task with video restoration followed by a spoken explanation. Identify which components and evaluation criteria change: temporal correspondence and fidelity enter, speech still needs pronunciation and interruption checks, and an explanatory model must not turn a plausible restored detail into an asserted fact. Explain which parts of your original architecture remain useful.',
'Finish with a maintenance plan and an honest completion record. In three days, repeat the architecture explanation without notes. In one week, repeat the diagnosis on a new example. Save unresolved questions and schedule targeted review. Marking this lesson reviewed records your self-assessment; it does not certify mastery or replace independent feedback. The full course materials are available, while continued practice determines how reliably you can apply them.'
],question:'Which final deliverable best supports a claim that the capstone works within its stated scope?',options:['One polished answer','A model name and parameter count','Runnable code, reproducible evaluation, failure demonstrations, and explicit limitations'],correct:2,explanation:'A working system needs evidence about its behavior, including boundaries and failures. A fluent example or model size alone cannot establish reliability.',prompt:'Deliver a five-minute oral defense, run the capstone suite, and write a release record covering data, model choices, runtime, evaluation, rollback, and one unresolved risk.',rubric:'Trace the full system; distinguish training from inference; defend provenance and split; compare a baseline; report metrics with denominators and assumptions; demonstrate refusal and interruption; explain cache invalidation and rollback; transfer the design to video plus speech; schedule recall.',recall:'Repeat the defense after three days and one week using a different application, then revisit the weakest explanation.'}
);

// Equations sit after the related paragraph; spoken versions define the notation.
const equationCards=[
  [2,1,'Prediction and loss','ŷ = xw\nL = ½(y − ŷ)²\nx = 2, w = 1, y = 6 ⇒ ŷ = 2, L = 8','Prediction equals input x times weight w. Loss is half the square of target y minus prediction. With input two, weight one, and target six, prediction is two and loss is eight.'],
  [2,2,'One learning step','w′ = w + ηx(y − xw)\nw′ = 1 + 0.1 × 2 × (6 − 2) = 1.8\nL′ = ½(6 − 3.6)² = 2.88','The new weight is the old weight plus learning rate eta times input times prediction error. Eta is zero point one. The new weight is one point eight, the new prediction is three point six, and the new loss is two point eight eight.'],
  [3,2,'Autoregressive factorization','P(t₁,…,tₙ) = ∏ᵢ₌₁ⁿ P(tᵢ | t₁,…,tᵢ₋₁)','The probability of a token sequence is the product of each token’s conditional probability given the preceding tokens. At the first token, the preceding sequence is empty, or supplied as a fixed prompt. Tokens are indexed by i, and n is the sequence length.'],
  [5,2,'Reconstruction error','MSE = (1/N) Σᵢ₌₁ᴺ (ŷᵢ − yᵢ)²','Mean squared error averages squared differences between predicted samples and aligned reference samples. N is the number of samples. Smaller pixel error is not proof of perceptual quality or recovered truth.'],
  [7,2,'Dot product and affine prediction','x = [2, 3], w = [4, −1], b = 1\nx · w = Σᵢ xᵢwᵢ = 2 × 4 + 3 × (−1) = 5\nŷ = x · w + b = 6','Multiply matching vector entries and add: two times four plus three times minus one gives five. Add bias b, equal to one, to get prediction six. x denotes features and w denotes weights.'],
  [8,1,'Negative log likelihood','L = −ln p(target)\n−ln(0.5) ≈ 0.693; −ln(0.25) ≈ 1.386','For a single categorical target, loss is minus the natural logarithm of the probability assigned to that target. Probability one half gives about zero point six nine three; one quarter gives about one point three eight six.'],
  [8,2,'Gradient descent','L(w) = ½(xw − y)²\n∂L/∂w = x(xw − y)\nw′ = w − η ∂L/∂w\nx = 2, w = 1, y = 6 ⇒ ∂L/∂w = −8','The derivative of half squared error with respect to weight is input times the difference between prediction and target. Subtract learning rate eta times this derivative to update the weight. In our example the derivative is minus eight, so a small positive learning rate increases the weight.'],
  [9,1,'Four-hidden-unit network','hⱼ = tanh(wⱼx + bⱼ), j = 1,…,4\nŷ = c + Σⱼ₌₁⁴ vⱼhⱼ','Hidden unit j applies the hyperbolic tangent to its weighted scalar input plus its bias. The prediction adds output bias c to the sum of hidden activations h times output weights v. This is the nonlinear network used in the lab.'],
  [10,1,'Evaluate a mean baseline','ȳtrain = (1/Ntrain) Σᵢ yᵢ\nMSEtest = (1/Ntest) Σⱼ (ŷⱼ − yⱼ)²','The mean baseline predicts the average training target for every test input. Compute test mean squared error using only held-out predictions and targets. N train and N test are their respective sample counts. Do not compute the baseline mean from test labels.'],
  [12,2,'Check cross-split leakage','Gtrain ∩ Gtest = ∅\nDtrain ∩ Dtest = ∅','For a group-isolated and exact-deduplicated split, the training and test group sets have an empty intersection, and the sets of normalized document texts also have an empty intersection. G denotes source groups and D denotes normalized texts. These are separate checks; neither excludes every kind of leakage.'],
  [13,2,'Add-one smoothing','P(next | fox) = [count(fox,next) + 1] / [count(fox) + V]\nP(sleeps | fox) = (2 + 1)/(3 + 7) = 0.3','Add one to the transition count and divide by the total outgoing count plus vocabulary size V. There are two fox-to-sleeps transitions, three outgoing fox transitions, and seven possible next tokens including the end marker, giving probability zero point three.'],
  [13,3,'Perplexity','PPL = exp[−(1/T) Σₜ₌₁ᵀ ln P(tₜ | contextₜ)]','Perplexity is the exponential of average negative log probability over T scored tokens. Compare values only under compatible tokenization and evaluation protocols. The tiny lab’s sentence score illustrates the calculation; it is not an independent generalization benchmark.'],
  [14,1,'Scaled dot-product attention','Attention(Q,K,V) = softmax(QKᵀ/√dₖ + M)V\nMᵢⱼ = −∞ for forbidden positions; otherwise 0','Multiply queries Q by transposed keys K, divide scores by the square root of key dimension, add mask M, and apply softmax across each row. Multiply these weights by values V. Negative infinity at a forbidden position gives it zero attention weight.'],
  [15,1,'Effective batch and memory','Beffective = Bmicro × accumulation steps × workers\n4 × 4 × 8 = 128 examples\nM = P × 16 bytes; P = 1,000,000 ⇒ M ≈ 15.26 MiB','Effective batch is microbatch per worker times accumulation steps times data-parallel workers. The example gives one hundred twenty-eight examples. Under the lab’s assumed sixteen bytes of parameter and optimizer state per parameter, one million parameters use about fifteen point two six mebibytes. Activations and other overhead are additional.'],
  [16,2,'Momentum checkpoint state','vₜ₊₁ = 0.9vₜ + (wₜ − 3)\nwₜ₊₁ = wₜ − 0.1vₜ₊₁','In the lab’s scalar momentum example, the new velocity is zero point nine times the old velocity plus weight minus three. Subtract zero point one times the new velocity from the weight. Restore both weight and velocity to reproduce the uninterrupted run.'],
  [17,2,'Low-rank update','W′ = W + BA\nB shape: dout × r; A shape: r × din\ntrainable entries = r(din + dout)\nr = 1, din = dout = 4 ⇒ 8 entries','Keep base matrix W fixed and add the product of factors B and A. B has output dimension by rank entries; A has rank by input dimension entries. Rank r limits the update capacity. A rank-one update to a four-by-four matrix uses eight trainable entries, versus sixteen in the base. The lab uses no extra scaling factor.'],
  [18,2,'Illustrative preference loss','L(m) = ln(1 + exp(−m))\nL(0) ≈ 0.693; L(1) ≈ 0.313','The illustrative logistic loss is the natural log of one plus the exponential of negative preference margin m. Increasing the preferred margin from zero to one reduces loss from about zero point six nine three to zero point three one three. This scalar illustration is not full language-model preference training.'],
  [19,1,'Temperature sampling','pᵢ = exp(zᵢ/τ) / Σⱼ exp(zⱼ/τ), τ > 0','For positive temperature tau, divide each logit z by temperature, exponentiate, and normalize over candidate tokens. Lower temperature concentrates probability on larger logits. Greedy decoding selects a largest logit directly; the displayed formula does not divide by a zero temperature.'],
  [19,3,'KV-cache estimate','bytes = 2 × layers × batch × tokens × KV heads × head size × bytes/value\n2 × 32 × 1 × 2048 × 8 × 128 × 2 = 268,435,456 bytes = 256 MiB','Count both keys and values, then multiply layer count, batch size, context tokens, key-value heads, head dimension, and bytes per value. The stated assumptions yield two hundred fifty-six mebibytes for two thousand forty-eight tokens. Four times as many tokens requires four times the cache memory under these assumptions.'],
  [20,1,'Symmetric weight quantization','s = 0.9/7\nq = clip(round(w/s), −7, 7)\nŵ = sq','Set scale s to zero point nine divided by seven. Divide weight w by the scale, round, and clamp to the integer range minus seven through seven. Reconstruct by multiplying the integer q by the scale. The lab uses this symmetric range and reports reconstruction error; it does not measure inference speed.'],
  [21,1,'Cosine similarity','cosine(q,d) = (q · d)/(‖q‖₂ ‖d‖₂)','For nonzero query vector q and document vector d, cosine similarity divides their dot product by the product of their Euclidean lengths. This explains a common embedding comparison. The runnable assistant instead counts overlapping terms, so its displayed score is not cosine similarity.'],
  [23,1,'Waveform storage','samples = sample rate × seconds\n24,000 × 10 = 240,000 samples\nPCM bytes = samples × channels × bytes/sample\n240,000 × 1 × 2 = 480,000 bytes','Ten seconds at twenty-four thousand samples per second contains two hundred forty thousand samples per channel. Mono sixteen-bit PCM uses two bytes per sample, totaling four hundred eighty thousand sample bytes. File headers require extra space.'],
  [23,3,'Word error rate','WER = (S + D + I)/Nreference\n1 substitution / 4 reference words = 0.25','Word error rate adds substitutions S, deletions D, and insertions I, then divides by reference word count. One substitution among four reference words gives twenty-five percent. Insertions can make word error rate exceed one. An empty reference requires an explicitly defined convention; the lab returns no numeric rate.'],
  [24,2,'Sequential latency budget','Tfirst sound = Trecognition + Tretrieval + Tsynthesis + Tplayback\n120 + 15 + 400 + 20 = 555 ms','For the lab’s illustrative non-overlapping stages, add recognition, retrieval, synthesis, and first-playback delay to get five hundred fifty-five milliseconds. Streaming stages can overlap, so real first-sound latency must be measured on the actual pipeline.'],
  [25,2,'Forward diffusion corruption','xₜ = √ᾱₜ x₀ + √(1 − ᾱₜ) ε\nε ~ N(0,I), 0 ≤ ᾱₜ ≤ 1','A common forward diffusion construction mixes clean data x zero with independent standard Gaussian noise epsilon. The cumulative signal coefficient alpha bar controls their square-root weights at time t. This is the noising process used in training, not the complete reverse sampling algorithm.'],
  [26,3,'Pixel fidelity and PSNR','MSE = (1/N) Σᵢ (ŷᵢ − yᵢ)²\nPSNR = 10 log₁₀(MAX²/MSE) dB\nMSE = 0.125, MAX = 1 ⇒ PSNR ≈ 9.03 dB','Peak signal-to-noise ratio is ten times the base-ten logarithm of peak sample value squared divided by mean squared error. With normalized peak one and error zero point one two five, the result is about nine point zero three decibels. Zero error gives infinite PSNR in the ideal formula. State the peak scale and align references; PSNR alone does not measure temporal coherence.'],
  [27,2,'Precision and recall','precision = TP/(TP + FP)\nrecall = TP/(TP + FN)','True positives are correctly predicted positives. Precision divides true positives by all predicted positives, including false positives. Recall divides true positives by all actual positives, including false negatives. Define which outcome counts as positive and specify how zero denominators are handled.'],
  [27,3,'Nearest-rank percentile','rank = ceil(pN)\nP95 = sorted durations[ceil(0.95 × 20)]\nrank = 19 ⇒ P95 = 28 ms; maximum = 500 ms','Using one-based indexing, the nearest-rank percentile selects the ceiling of percentile fraction p times sample count N. With twenty durations, the ninety-fifth percentile is the nineteenth ordered value, twenty-eight milliseconds. The twentieth value, five hundred milliseconds, is the maximum.']
];
for(const [lesson,after,title,expression,spoken] of equationCards){
  (lessons[lesson-1].equations??=[]).push({after,title,expression,spoken});
}
